
[FIXED] Volatile partition and rendered climate now share the gray radiation calculation. The temperature solver couples gas saturation, climate-dependent weathering, and cold nitrogen absorption; conserves the retained reservoirs; preserves supplied temperature/composition constraints; and records convergence residuals and detected alternative equilibria. An explicit mixed-column vapor factor replaces the former surface-humidity proxy. Runaway greenhouse and evolving ice feedback remain separate tasks below.

[FIXED] The temperature estimator does not resolve runaway greenhouse. It calculates warming from prescribed gas abundances. A finite, comfortable answer therefore does not establish that evaporation and atmospheric feedback would preserve that state. We should add checks to determine whether it would.

[FIXED] Seasonal water ice now evolves stored energy and ice thickness, spends latent heat before warming meltwater, conducts heat through the ice, and couples reflected sunlight to freezing and melting. Orbital eccentricity and axial seasons drive the model independently. Continuous updates preserve thermal history; explicit time jumps reconstruct the orbital history; checkpoints retain energy and accept older saves. Background preparation records seasonal convergence and the assumed depth, reflectivity, and cloud masking. This remains a representative-column model with a prescribed atmosphere, without snow accumulation, ice transport, or melt-pond drainage; an unconverged seasonal cycle is flagged rather than establishing a recurring opening.

[FIXED] Seasonal ice reconstruction formerly omitted regional climate variations from its stored energy, producing straight latitude boundaries. Regional cold and warm histories now carry those variations through freezing, melting, and reflectivity feedback, with geographic variation on several spatial scales. Small ongoing changes in orbital geometry also formerly invalidated background histories repeatedly; geometric cache tolerances now allow publication while retaining the current orbital phase. Regression checks cover irregular map margins and changing polar ice on a precessing Earth-like world with an advancing clock.

[FIXED] Seasonal ice could change thickness without visibly opening water. The ice calculation incorrectly reflected redistributed heat along with sunlight, used optical whiteness as an overly abrupt climatic albedo transition, and interpolated between permanent ice and open ocean without resolving intermediate seasonal climates. Transported heat is now separate from reflected sunlight, climatic albedo uses a distinct 0.5 m transition scale, and temperature sampling refines freezing transitions to 0.1 K. Superseded checkpoints reconstruct their current-date energy before publication. Tests require ice, open water, and renewed ice at the same map pixels under both axial and eccentric seasons, plus matching changes in a cached GPU texture.

[FIXED] Accelerated clock playback repeatedly treated large time ticks as explicit seeks, canceling background work and restarting the geographic sweep before most tiles could publish. Planet updates now preserve sweep progress during playback, while explicit date controls still invalidate stale work. Intervals that skip an orbital sample reconstruct the resolved energy history. A playback regression checks every geographic tile and repeated freezing, thawing, and refreezing while the date advances on every frame.

Atmospheric haze does not dim with eclipses like the terrain, water, vegetation, and clouds do. Fix the eclipse effects so they dim the atmosphere too, and any other visible local components that would be dimmed in real life. Obviously do not dim stars, galaxies, other planets/moons, etc.

